-field/id #uuid "cd7c56fa-7567-5c85-b0fb-c825f694718e" :answer-field/key "field-9" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443403} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e882974-a608-5b59-80b3-81a5ab2ccd4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443323} {:answer/id #uuid "27a95bd3-7a95-5ddc-ad8e-1cefe25e8a3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443330} {:answer/id #uuid "d910eab7-7132-5df5-b3c3-2fcc4066ae76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27a95bd3-7a95-5ddc-ad8e-1cefe25e8a3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443330} :answer-field/id #uuid "d28e5083-6111-5659-843b-b5cd8118683f" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c1d729e8-d1cb-584b-b25e-2da8d463c255" :question/math-academy-id "q-363831" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to \n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | ${x}_{2}$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince the two linearly independent solutions are already mutually orthogonal, we only need to normalize them. Hence, we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-6}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-7}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                $0$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162712 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162043 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b36c482-75a0-5413-8e3e-4a72e3ddb26a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46ec70b2-790d-582b-8940-37eea292ae4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430279} {:answer/id #uuid "4d30fcb9-4e9c-5480-91f8-be9027ba4304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430281} {:answer/id #uuid "5ea213a0-7cb0-59f5-98bd-30dcad60aa90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430287} {:answer/id #uuid "84575063-53ca-5b2a-94b3-9e965df7c601" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430299} {:answer/id #uuid "d346a1ab-d6ee-5497-ae67-93ca1862ead9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d346a1ab-d6ee-5497-ae67-93ca1862ead9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430325} :answer-field/id #uuid "53075006-3bde-5335-a07e-7b2f11fef0a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0acab5c5-194e-580b-922b-276032b93c12" :question/math-academy-id "q-97365" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the $3$rd column of the matrix $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=3$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st and $2$nd columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,‖{u}_{3}‖=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the nonzero normalized solution \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U:$\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162043 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b36c482-75a0-5413-8e3e-4a72e3ddb26a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fd0fef2-41cf-5b78-ab4f-6f03d4dacfd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161643} {:answer/id #uuid "17be549c-a1c7-5060-987e-c5b65ac2b51a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161644} {:answer/id #uuid "3bcd3583-93e7-5a4f-bd1e-dbffb277553f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161645} {:answer/id #uuid "56db7078-0264-534f-afb4-fd027ef181b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161646} {:answer/id #uuid "6402e77e-c348-5104-b7eb-d18e02ab6813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161647}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a8478cb6-55ac-5ba0-aa86-521b99077edf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161642} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e50a614d-fd6f-55be-9356-ebb15749bb1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382663} {:answer/id #uuid "f1a31908-a2f5-584e-8101-a70a1d5408a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382664} {:answer/id #uuid "5fa7355e-413d-5cfe-860d-bd5a74b01f14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382665} {:answer/id #uuid "d2048545-37eb-5154-98fb-fbdc70936239" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382666} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ccc71c-705b-55f1-bd7a-216a4ecad2d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3ccc71c-705b-55f1-bd7a-216a4ecad2d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382667} :answer-field/id #uuid "601494e0-9a29-5b8f-8afd-34cbf959efa1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dcd0e8b8-71e5-5dcd-a370-0847d15e9434" :question/math-academy-id "q-98797" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the $3$rd column of the matrix $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=6$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st and $2$nd columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,‖{u}_{3}‖=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the nonzero normalized solution \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U:$\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}]}
